            formulaHighlight = "✔ KEY POINT — Mendel used seven pairs of contrasting traits in pea, built true-breeding lines by repeated self-pollination, and worked 1856 to 1863; inheritance is particulate — factors (alleles) do not blend.\n✔ KEY POINT — Monohybrid F2: phenotypic ratio 3:1, genotypic ratio 1 TT : 2 Tt : 1 tt; the test cross (unknown dominant crossed with recessive) gives all dominant offspring from TT and a 1:1 split from Tt.\n✔ KEY POINT — Law of Dominance: in a dissimilar pair of factors, one dominates and the other stays recessive and hidden. Law of Segregation: alleles separate unchanged at gamete formation, so each gamete carries only one allele and is pure for the trait.\n✔ KEY POINT — Incomplete dominance: heterozygote intermediate (snapdragon and Mirabilis pink), F2 phenotypic ratio 1:2:1 equal to the genotypic ratio. Codominance: both alleles fully expressed side by side, as IA IB giving blood group AB.\n✔ KEY POINT — ABO blood groups: gene I with three alleles IA, IB, i; six genotypes, four phenotypes; IA and IB dominant over i and codominant with each other. A group O child proves both parents carry i.\n✔ KEY POINT — Multiple alleles: three or more forms of one gene in the population, two per individual. Pleiotropy: one gene with many phenotypic effects (sickle-cell; pea starch grains B and b). Polygenic inheritance: many genes, one quantitative trait (skin colour; wheat kernel colour), dominant alleles adding up.\n✔ KEY POINT — Dihybrid F2 phenotypic ratio 9:3:3:1; Law of Independent Assortment: segregation of one character pair is independent of the other at gamete formation; dihybrid test cross gives 1:1:1:1.\n✔ KEY POINT — Chromosomal theory (Sutton and Boveri, 1902): genes lie on chromosomes whose meiotic behaviour parallels Mendel's factors. Morgan verified it in Drosophila; white eye on the X was the first gene located on a chromosome; Sturtevant built the first genetic map.\n✔ KEY POINT — Linkage: genes on one chromosome inherited together; recombination by crossing over creates non-parental combinations. Morgan's numbers: yellow-white 98.7 percent parental, 1.3 percent recombinant; white-miniature 62.8 percent parental, 37.2 percent recombinant. One percent recombination equals one map unit; linkage groups equal the haploid chromosome number.\n✔ KEY POINT — Sex determination: XX-XY (humans, Drosophila — male heterogametic), ZZ-ZW (birds — female heterogametic), XX-XO (grasshopper — male has one X less), haplodiploidy (honeybee — females diploid 32 from fertilised eggs, males haploid 16 from unfertilised eggs by parthenogenesis).\n✔ KEY POINT — Mutation alters DNA sequence: point mutation changes one base (sickle-cell, GAG to GUG, Glu to Val); frameshift insertions or deletions shift the reading frame; non-disjunction causes aneuploidy. Mutagens like UV raise the rate.\n✔ KEY POINT — Pedigree rules: affected child of unaffected parents means recessive; affected daughter of unaffected father excludes X-linked recessive; X-linked recessive shows far more affected males and no father-to-son passage; NCERT's pair is myotonic dystrophy (autosomal dominant) and sickle-cell anaemia (autosomal recessive).\n✔ KEY POINT — Mendelian disorders: haemophilia (X-linked recessive, clotting fails); colour blindness (X-linked recessive, about 8 percent males, 0.4 percent females); sickle-cell anaemia (autosomal recessive, HbS HbS affected, HbA HbS carrier); phenylketonuria (autosomal recessive, phenylalanine hydroxylase missing); thalassemia (autosomal recessive, alpha genes HBA1 and HBA2 on chromosome 16, beta gene HBB on chromosome 11).\n✔ KEY POINT — Chromosomal disorders: Down's syndrome (trisomy 21, 47 chromosomes, described by Langdon Down in 1866); Klinefelter's (47, XXY, sterile males with gynaecomastia); Turner's (45, XO, sterile females with rudimentary ovaries).",
