            formulaHighlight = "✔ KEY POINT — Dates of the stage: Earth formed about 4.5 billion years ago; life appeared about 4 billion years ago (500 million years later); first cellular forms about 2000 million years ago.\n✔ KEY POINT — Oparin and Haldane: life arose from pre-existing non-living organic molecules, preceded by chemical evolution. Miller (1953) tested it: CH4, H2, NH3 and water vapour, electric discharge at 800 degrees Celsius, and amino acids formed.\n✔ KEY POINT — Homologous organs: same structure and origin, different function — vertebrate forelimbs; Bougainvillea thorn and Cucurbita tendril. They record divergent evolution from a common ancestor.\n✔ KEY POINT — Analogous organs: different structure and origin, same function — butterfly and bird wings; octopus and mammal eyes; penguin and dolphin flippers; sweet potato (root) and potato (stem). They record convergent evolution.\n✔ KEY POINT — Embryology: Haeckel proposed the support (embryos share features adults lack, like gill slits); von Baer corrected it — embryos resemble other embryos, never adult stages of other animals. Biochemical evidence: proteins and genes similar across lineages.\n✔ KEY POINT — Adaptive radiation: one ancestor, many niches — Darwin's Galapagos finches from a seed-eating stock; Australian marsupials from one ancestral stock. Placental-marsupial parallels: wolf with Tasmanian wolf, flying squirrel with flying phalanger, mole with marsupial mole.\n✔ KEY POINT — Industrial melanism (Biston bitularia): collections of the 1850s held more white-winged moths; by 1920, after soot darkened bark and killed lichens, dark-winged moths dominated. Selection acted on pre-existing variation — directional selection.\n✔ KEY POINT — Anthropogenic evolution: excess antibiotics, herbicides and pesticides select resistant strains within months or years. Evolution is a stochastic process based on chance events in nature and chance mutations in organisms.\n✔ KEY POINT — The theorists: Lamarck — use and disuse, inheritance of acquired characters (giraffe's neck), rejected. Darwin — branching descent and natural selection (HMS Beagle, Galapagos). Wallace — Malay Archipelago, same conclusion independently. De Vries — evening primrose, large directionless mutations, saltation.\n✔ KEY POINT — Hardy-Weinberg: allele frequencies stay constant at equilibrium; genotype frequencies follow p^2 + 2pq + q^2 = 1 with p + q = 1. The five disturbers: gene flow, genetic drift (including the founder effect), mutation, genetic recombination, natural selection.\n✔ KEY POINT — Selection patterns: stabilising — more individuals acquire the mean value; directional — the peak shifts; disruptive — more individuals acquire peripheral values at both ends.\n✔ KEY POINT — Timeline anchors: invertebrates active by about 500 mya; jawless fish about 350 mya; lobefins about 350 mya (coelacanth caught 1938, South Africa); ichthyosaurs and dinosaur rise about 200 mya; dinosaurs disappear about 65 mya; Tyrannosaurus about 20 feet tall.\n✔ KEY POINT — Human ladder: Dryopithecus (ape-like) and Ramapithecus (man-like) about 15 mya; Australopithecus about 2 mya, East African grasslands, 3-4 feet, upright, stone weapons, mostly fruit; Homo habilis 650-800 cc, probably no meat; Homo erectus about 1.5 mya, about 900 cc, ate meat; Neanderthal 1400 cc, 100,000-40,000 years ago, hides and burials; Homo sapiens arose in Africa, modern form in the ice age 75,000-10,000 years ago; cave art about 18,000 years ago; agriculture about 10,000 years ago.",
