rsons against air-borne ones; vector control against mosquito-borne ones — no stagnant water, insecticides in ditches and swamps, Gambusia in ponds, wire mesh on windows.\n✔ Innate immunity (from birth, non-specific), four barriers: physical — skin and mucus coatings; physiological — stomach acid, saliva, tears; cellular — PMNL-neutrophils, monocytes, macrophages, natural killer cells; cytokine — interferons protecting non-infected cells.\n✔ Acquired immunity is pathogen-specific with memory: primary response low-intensity; secondary (anamnestic) response highly intensified via memory B-cells and T-cells. B-cells produce antibodies, T-cells help. Active: own antibodies after antigen — slow, lasting (vaccination). Passive: ready-made antibodies — immediate, brief; colostrum IgA, placental IgG.\n✔ Antibody: four peptide chains, two light and two heavy — H2L2 — a Y with antigen-binding sites at the arm tips. Class addresses: IgA in colostrum, IgG across the placenta, IgE in allergy; IgM is the fourth class named.\n✔ Vaccination introduces antigenic proteins or inactivated or weakened pathogens; the body makes antibodies and memory B-cells and T-cells. Hepatitis B vaccine is produced from yeast by recombinant DNA technology. Antivenom is passive immunisation, not vaccination.\n✔ Allergy: exaggerated response to allergens; IgE antibodies; mast cells release histamine and serotonin; sneezing, running nose, breathing difficulty; anti-histamine, adrenaline and steroids relieve it. Autoimmunity: body attacks self cells; example rheumatoid arthritis.\n✔ Lymphoid organs: primary — bone marrow (all blood cells produced, B-cells mature) and thymus (T-cells mature, shrinks with age); secondary — spleen (blood filter, erythrocyte reservoir), lymph nodes (trap lymph antigens), tonsils, Peyer's patches, appendix; MALT is about 50 percent of lymphoid tissue.\n✔ AIDS: HIV, a retrovirus with RNA genome; in macrophages, reverse transcriptase copies RNA into viral DNA, which joins the host genome — the macrophage becomes an HIV factory; helper T-lymphocytes progressively decrease; diagnosed by ELISA; spreads only via body fluids — sex, contaminated blood, shared needles, placenta — never by touch; no permanent cure.\n✔ Cancer: loss of contact inhibition; benign tumors stay confined, malignant tumors invade and metastasise via blood; carcinogens are ionising radiations and UV, tobacco-smoke chemicals, oncogenic viruses; normal cells carry proto-oncogenes (c-onc); detection by biopsy, blood counts, radiography, CT (X-rays, 3D) and MRI (magnetic fields, non-ionising); treatment includes surgery, radiation, chemotherapy and alpha-interferon.\n✔ Drugs: opioids bind receptors in the CNS and gut — morphine from poppy latex (Papaver somniferum), heroin is diacetylmorphine, a depressant; cannabinoids from Cannabis sativa, known for cardiovascular effects; cocaine from Erythroxylum coca interferes with dopamine transport; nicotine makes the adrenal gland release adrenaline and nor-adrenaline; dependence brings tolerance and withdrawal.",
