            formulaHighlight = "✔ KEY POINT — An ecosystem is a functional unit where biotic organisms interact among themselves and with the abiotic environment; structure = species composition + stratification (vertical layering), function = productivity, decomposition, energy flow and nutrient cycling. Pond crew: producers — phytoplankton, algae, floating, submerged, marginal plants; consumers — zooplankton, free-swimming, bottom-dwelling animals; decomposers — fungi, bacteria, flagellates, at the bottom.\n✔ KEY POINT — GPP is the total rate of production of organic matter in photosynthesis; NPP = GPP − R (R = respiration losses) is the biomass available to heterotrophs; secondary productivity is the consumers' rate of formation of new organic matter. Biosphere NPP is about 170 billion tons (dry weight) yearly — oceans 55 billion tons from about 70 per cent of the surface, land 115.\n✔ KEY POINT — Decomposition breaks detritus (dead plant and animal remains, faecal matter) into carbon dioxide, water and nutrients; it is largely oxygen-requiring. Five steps in order: fragmentation, leaching, catabolism, humification, mineralisation. Detritivores (earthworm) fragment; bacteria and fungi digest; humus — dark, amorphous, colloidal, decay-resistant — is a nutrient reservoir.\n✔ KEY POINT — Decomposition is slower for lignin- and chitin-rich detritus, quicker for nitrogen- and sugar-rich detritus. Temperature and soil moisture are the main climatic controls: warm and moist favours decomposition; low temperature and anaerobiosis inhibit it.\n✔ KEY POINT — Energy flow is unidirectional (sun to producers to consumers to heat); ecosystems need constant input, and the sun powers all ecosystems except the deep-sea hydrothermal system. Under 50 per cent of incident solar radiation is PAR; plants capture only 2 to 10 per cent of the PAR — the fraction sustaining the entire living world. Nutrients cycle; energy does not.\n✔ KEY POINT — The grazing food chain starts from producers and is the major energy conduit in aquatic ecosystems; the detritus food chain starts from dead organic matter and carries the larger energy fraction on land. Levels: producers T1, herbivores T2, carnivores T3, top carnivores T4; standing crop is the living mass at a level at a time.\n✔ KEY POINT — Lindeman's ten per cent law: only 10 per cent of energy transfers from one trophic level to the next, the rest lost largely as heat — so energy decreases at successive levels and grazing food chains rarely exceed four or five levels.\n✔ KEY POINT — Pyramids of number, biomass and energy are upright in most ecosystems, but numbers can bulge on a single tree and biomass is inverted in the sea, where tiny phytoplankton turn over very fast. The energy pyramid is always upright; pyramids ignore multi-level species, food webs and decomposers.\n✔ KEY POINT — Succession is the gradual, predictable change in species composition, from pioneer species through seral stages to a climax community in near equilibrium with the environment. Hydrarch (water; phytoplankton pioneers) and xerarch (bare rock; lichen pioneers) converge on mesic conditions and forest; secondary succession on soil-bearing sites is much faster than primary.\n✔ KEY POINT — Carbon cycle (gaseous type): 71 per cent of global carbon is dissolved in the oceans, which regulate atmospheric CO2; the atmosphere holds only about 1 per cent; photosynthesis fixes nearly 4 × 10^13 kg annually; respiration, decomposition, burning and volcanic activity return it.\n✔ KEY POINT — Phosphorus cycle (sedimentary type): reservoir is phosphate rock; rainfall inputs are much smaller than carbon's and gaseous exchanges are negligible — no respiratory release of phosphorus. Costanza valued ecosystem services at about 33 trillion US dollars yearly, nearly twice the global GNP of about 18 trillion, soil formation about 50 per cent.",
